On October 1, 2026, AlixLabs began a six-month evaluation of its atomic-layer-etch pitch-splitting process in imec's NanoIC pilot line. The plan starts with coupons, then transfers selected conditions to full wafers and measures dimensions, roughness, profile and stochastic defects. That sequence preserves expensive full-wafer work for settings that survive earlier screening.

On November 11, imec and Vertical Compute announced an expanded engineering partnership to move a vertically integrated memory concept toward a 16-nanometer pre-production chip. The commercial foundry will make the base chip, while imec will add the proprietary memory layer in its pilot line.

These are current learning programs, not proof of stable high-volume output. AlixLabs still needs full-wafer evidence, equipment qualification and manufacturer adoption. Vertical Compute describes a path toward production, not shipped volume. Their relevance is the staged mechanism: experiments reduce uncertainty before larger manufacturing commitments.

The biggest takeaway is that production and engineering lots compete for the same physical resources but create different kinds of value. Production lots monetize today's qualified process. Well-designed engineering lots can improve tomorrow's yield, release new capacity or prevent a larger qualification failure. The right question is not whether engineering interrupts output; it is whether a small, controlled interruption buys enough reliable learning to improve the much larger stream that follows.

